**Correction to my two earlier comments on this issue.** They say in bold to write `srv.Handler = ingressHandler.Mount(mux)`. **`Mount` no longer exists**, and the advice is now wrong in a way that would cost time: there is no `*Handler` type, no exported pattern and no exported `ServeHTTP`.

The shape as of #47:

```go
h, err := ingress.New(reg, hub, ingress.Options{
    PublicBaseURL: cfg.PublicBaseURL,
    Routes: []ingress.Route{
        {Method: "GET", Path: "/healthz", Handler: healthz},
    },
})
if err != nil { return err }
srv.Handler = h
```

`ingress.New` owns the mux and returns the only handler that exists, so the trap my earlier comments warned about is gone by construction rather than by care: there is no bare mux to serve by mistake. Every other route this item mounts, including the `/v1` operator API when it arrives, goes in as a `Route`, and `cmd/lawang/serve.go`'s own `newMux()` goes away when you wire this.

`New` validates the routes and turns a `net/http` pattern panic into a start-up error, so a wiring mistake fails at start rather than in a request goroutine. Everything else on this issue stands, including that B07 should refuse to start when a registered provider's scheme needs `LAWANG_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` and none is configured.

Fixed in `581b862`, by refusing the prefix as you suggested rather than by narrowing the description.

`Route.check` now refuses any path under the webhook endpoint's own prefix:

```go
if strings.HasPrefix(r.Path, edgePrefix) {
	return fmt.Errorf("the path is under %s, which is the webhook endpoint this package serves itself (%s)", edgePrefix, routePath)
}
```

`edgePrefix` is `routePath[:strings.LastIndex(routePath, "/")+1]`, derived rather than written down a second time, so a change to the edge's route cannot leave the refusal behind. It also matches the grammar the tunnel in front of a development machine enforces on that one prefix, which is the other place this path shape is owned.

Three cases are in the refused table now: `a literal under the webhook endpoint` (`POST /ingress/fake`, the one that got through and answered in the edge's place), `a subtree under the webhook endpoint` (`/ingress/{rest...}`, which already left the edge answering and is refused with it because nothing legitimate lives there), and `the webhook pattern itself`, which used to be caught only by the duplicate-pattern check. Deleting the branch fails the first two, and each table in `TestNoRouteIsEverAnsweredWithARedirect` still ends by asserting the edge answers 202.

What I did not do is try to catch every pattern that could reach `/ingress/x` from outside the prefix, because `net/http`'s own rules already do: `/{a}/{b}` is less specific than `POST /ingress/{provider}` so the edge keeps answering, and `/{a}/fake` overlaps it with neither more specific, so the mux refuses the table and `New` turns that into an error. The prefix rule covers what those two do not.

**Disputed**, with the test you asked for added anyway in `1c9129e`.

I built exactly the case you described and it does not distinguish the mutant. Routes `{Path: "/a/x/"}`, `{Path: "/b/y/"}`, `{Method: "GET", Path: "/b/{z}"}`: `/a/x` is needed and registers fine, `/b/y` is needed and conflicts with `GET /b/{z}`. The message names `/b/y` both with the line and without it.

The reason is that `guarding` is not stale at the point of the panic. It is assigned unconditionally at the top of the block, immediately before the only two calls in `newMux` that can panic while it is read:

```go
guarding = Route{Method: rt.Method, Path: root}.pattern()
mux.Handle(guarding, ...)   // panics here
probe.Handle(guarding, nothing)
guarding = ""
```

So a second guard's panic reports the second guard's name whether or not the clear happens. For the clear to matter, something would have to panic after a successful guard and before the next assignment. The only candidates in the deferred region are `redirectRoot` (pure string work) and `redirects`, which serves into a mux whose handlers are all `nothing`; every `mux.Handle` for a caller's route happens in the first loop, which finishes before `guarding` is ever set. I ran the mutation against the whole package after adding the test: still green.

So I read this one as an equivalent mutant, like M10 and M12, and I have marked it at the line rather than trying to pin it:

```go
// Cleared so that guarding names a route in flight and nothing else. Deleting this line
// changes no message and no answer, so that mutation is equivalent: guarding is assigned
// unconditionally just above, immediately before the only two calls in this function that
// can panic while it is read, and nothing between one guard and the next can panic. It
// stays because the invariant is what the deferred recover above is written against, and
// a panic site added after this loop would read a stale name without it.
```

The test stays regardless, because "a conflict in the second guard is reported under the second guard" was genuinely uncovered before (only the first-guard case at `ingress_test.go:1468` was), and it also asserts the message does **not** name `/a/x`. If you can construct a table where the stale value is observable, I will take it as a finding.

Fixed in 46148fd. Every value in your list is now in `keptPhones`, and the comment describes what the code does.

I went wider than the suggested IPv4 test, because your list is wider than IPv4: `2024.100.200`, `100.200.300`, `2026-0921-1234` and `123.456.789` are not addresses and were masked for the same reason. `validPhone` is the whole rule now and the pattern is explicitly only a shape. The national form (no country code) needs:

- nine to fifteen digits, as before;
- **exactly three groups**. Four is a dotted quad or a build number, and no national spelling uses four groups. This is what refuses every IPv4 address, whatever its octets, rather than only the ones with a 4-digit group;
- **at least one group of exactly four digits**. `0812-3456-7890`, `(555) 010-1234` and `555.010.1234` all have one; three groups of three is an address, an invoice total or a commit;
- **a first group that does not read as a year** (19xx or 20xx). That is the only thing separating `2026-0921-1234` from `0812-3456-7890`, which have the identical digit shape, so the rule is semantic and says so at the line.

What it gives up is a real number whose first group reads as a year, which then reaches the sink. The function comment and ADR 12 both state that trade and why it is the right way round: a miss is recoverable, an over-reach is not, because the sink never sees the original and the value is in `redaction_map`.

The international form is unchanged: a `+` is what makes grouped digits a number, so E.164's bounds are all it needs. `+<ip>` is a phone number and is tested as one.

`keptPhones` carries all eight of yours plus `<ip>` (refused by the pattern), `1999-0102-0304`, and the four classes you asked for: an order id, a version with a prefix, a hash and two timestamps. `TestThePhoneRuleIsEveryRuleThePatternCannotState` replaces the digit-count table with one pair per rule, each differing in that rule alone (`0812-3456-7890` vs `081-234-5678-90`, `100.200.300` vs `100.200.3000`, `2026-0921-1234` vs `2126-0921-1234`).

Mutations: restoring the round 1 `validPhone` kills the table and the fixtures; removing each of the three new rules on its own kills the table, and two of the three also kill the fixtures.
